  "subject": "chemistry",
  "title": "Reactions of metal ions in aqueous solution",
  "key_idea": "Metal ions in water exist as hexaaqua complexes. Sodium hydroxide and ammonia remove H⁺ from the water ligands, giving hydroxide precipitates. Some precipitates dissolve in excess: by amphoteric behaviour in excess NaOH, or by ligand exchange in excess NH₃. Ions with a higher charge density are more acidic.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "Metal-aqua ions and their acidity",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "In water, metal ions are surrounded by six water ligands. Some of these complexes behave as acids."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "columns": [
            "Aqua ion",
            "Colour of solution"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "[Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺",
              "Blue"
            ],
            [
              "[Fe(H₂O)₆]²⁺",
              "Pale green"
            ],
            [
              "[Fe(H₂O)₆]³⁺",
              "Yellow-brown"
            ],
            [
              "[Cr(H₂O)₆]³⁺",
              "Green"
            ],
            [
              "[Co(H₂O)₆]²⁺",
              "Pink"
            ],
            [
              "[Mn(H₂O)₆]²⁺",
              "Pale pink"
            ],
            [
              "[Al(H₂O)₆]³⁺",
              "Colourless"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "label": "Acid behaviour of an aqua ion",
          "formula": "[Fe(H₂O)₆]³⁺ + H₂O ⇌ [Fe(H₂O)₅(OH)]²⁺ + H₃O⁺",
          "where": [
            "A water ligand loses H⁺ to a water molecule in the solution."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "Solutions of 3+ ions are **more acidic** than solutions of 2+ ions. For example, Fe³⁺(aq) has a lower pH than Fe²⁺(aq).",
            "A 3+ ion has a higher charge and a smaller radius, so a higher **charge/size ratio**.",
            "It attracts the lone pairs on the water ligands more strongly. This weakens the O–H bonds, so H⁺ is released more easily."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "key-points",
          "items": [
            "Aqua ions are [M(H₂O)₆]²⁺ or [M(H₂O)₆]³⁺.",
            "3+ aqua ions are more acidic than 2+ ions.",
            "Cause: a higher charge/size ratio polarises O–H bonds in the water ligands more."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Reactions with sodium hydroxide and ammonia",
