    {
      "heading": "Amphoteric behaviour and ligand exchange",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "A precipitate can dissolve in excess for two different reasons. The equations show which."
        },
        {
          "type": "definitions",
          "items": [
            {
              "term": "Amphoteric",
              "meaning": "Able to react with both acids and bases. An amphoteric hydroxide dissolves in both."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "label": "Amphoteric: excess NaOH removes more H⁺ (acid-base)",
          "formula": "Cr(OH)₃(H₂O)₃ + 3OH⁻ → [Cr(OH)₆]³⁻ + 3H₂O",
          "where": [
            "Ligands stay the same number: water becomes hydroxide by losing H⁺.",
            "Al(OH)₃(H₂O)₃ + OH⁻ → [Al(OH)₄(H₂O)₂]⁻ + H₂O."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "label": "Ligand exchange: excess NH₃ replaces water (substitution)",
          "formula": "Cu(OH)₂(H₂O)₄ + 4NH₃ → [Cu(NH₃)₄(H₂O)₂]²⁺ + 2OH⁻ + 2H₂O",
          "where": [
            "NH₃ replaces water ligands and the hydroxide leaves."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "Amphoteric: H⁺ is removed from ligands. The ligands change from H₂O to OH⁻.",
            "Ligand exchange: one ligand is swapped for a different ligand, here NH₃ for H₂O.",
            "Chromium(III) hydroxide shows both: amphoteric with NaOH, ligand exchange with NH₃, giving [Cr(NH₃)₆]³⁺."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "subheading",
          "text": "Reaction with carbonate ions"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "M²⁺ ions (Fe, Cu) give a precipitate of the metal carbonate: FeCO₃ is green and CuCO₃ is blue-green.",
            "M³⁺ ions (Al, Fe) are acidic enough to react with carbonate ions and give carbon dioxide as well as a hydroxide precipitate.",
            "For example, 2[Al(H₂O)₆]³⁺ + 3CO₃²⁻ → 2Al(OH)₃(H₂O)₃ + 3CO₂ + 3H₂O. Bubbles and a white precipitate."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "key-points",
          "items": [
            "Amphoteric: hydroxide dissolves in excess base by losing more H⁺.",
            "Ligand exchange: NH₃ replaces water ligands.",
            "3+ ions with carbonate give CO₂ and a hydroxide precipitate."
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
